NOT APPLIED BY THE BUILD. Kept for the record (decision of 2026-11-12: CREST does not patch Chez; it
avoids the leak by never letting an error cross a C function, plans/04-errors-are-values.md).

Chez Scheme: free the records of the C calls that a Scheme error escapes
(c/schlib.c, S_call_help, "case 1: normal return")

THE LEAK, IN PLAIN WORDS
  Every call from C into Scheme (Scall0, Scall1, ... ) goes through S_call_help. It allocates a jump
  buffer (about 200 bytes), adds a record for the call to a list of the active C calls (CCHAIN), and
  locks the code object of the caller. When the call returns normally, S_call_help removes the record,
  frees the buffer and unlocks the code object.
  When a Scheme error is caught by a `guard` that lies OUTSIDE a C function that called into Scheme,
  the error escapes through that C function: its call never returns, so its buffer, its record and its
  lock stay. Stock Chez leaks about 208 bytes for every such error.

WHY (THE CODE)
  When the outer call finally returns, S_call_help has a loop whose comment says it unlocks the code
  objects "that we're escaping past" and frees their buffers: it walks the list from its head down to
  the outer call's own record. The line `CCHAIN(tc) = yp;` just before the loop cuts the list down to
  the outer call's own record first, so the loop has nothing else to walk. The comment of S_return
  says the same thing about what the return handler does ("will release frames not in the yp sublist").
  This patch deletes the line, so the loop starts at the real head of the list, as its comment says.

THE SMALLEST REPRO (standalone, no CREST): cmake/patches/chez-error-escape-leak/{repro.c,repro.ss}
  C:       call_scheme()  { call the Scheme procedure raise-it }       <- the C function in the middle
           main()         { loop 100000 times: call the Scheme procedure try }
  Scheme:  raise-it = (lambda () (error 'x "boom"))
           call-c   = (foreign-procedure "call_scheme" () void)        <- Scheme calls call_scheme
           try      = (lambda () (guard (c [#t #f]) (call-c)))         <- the guard is outside call_scheme
  Result: stock Chez "heap grew 20800000 bytes over 100000 errors (208.0 per error)"; patched: 0 bytes.
  Under valgrind the stock run reports "definitely lost ... malloc ... S_call_help".

HOW OUR TESTS HIT IT
  A Scheme error caught by a `guard` outside a C++ binding or callback, in a program that scheme-mlir
  or crest-opt started with a call from C into Scheme (that call is the outer frame).
  Measured 2026-11-12:
    valgrind over the 227 lit tests, with scheme-mlir and crest-opt linked against stock Chez. 32 tests
    leave records of escaped calls behind (about 208 bytes each), but valgrind does NOT report them as
    "lost": scheme-mlir and crest-opt run the whole program inside ONE call from C that has not returned
    when the process exits, so the records are still on Chez's list ("still reachable"). They are freed
    only when that outer call returns, so in these processes they are held until the end.
    - 21 tests have exactly one record: the error that ends the program (nothing to free).
    - 11 tests have more, i.e. errors that a guard caught: dsl/error-path-leak (6200, it raises on
      purpose), dsl/callback-leaks (909), dsl/interface-dsl/callable-clauses-errors (9),
      toy/ch7/undefined-function (5), dsl/dialect-traits/traits-errors-llvm23 (4), toy/ch4/driver (4), and
      2 each in toy/ch4/diagnostics, dsl/op-builders/infer-error, dsl/op-builders/regions,
      dsl/dialect-load-errors, dsl/op-traits/op-traits-errors.
    - Which call made the escaped frame: 7121 of the 7162 records come from scheme_error
      (lib/Bindings/Support/SchemeWrapper.h: a C++ binding raises, so the C function in the middle is the
      binding itself); 35 from other C++ calls into Scheme.
    Where the leak is a real loss: a host that calls Scheme again and again from C, each call returning
    (CrestInterpreterTests: 20000 calls, 4 MB lost before the patch), and any process in which the
    outermost call returns after errors were caught.
  CrestInterpreterTests, SchemeErrorDoesNotLeakCppMemory, is the same scenario as the repro with 20000
  errors: it fails (about 200 bytes per error) if this patch is not applied.

UPSTREAM
  Not fixed upstream: the same line is in cisco/ChezScheme main (d533f2e, 2026-11-07) and in
  racket/ChezScheme master, and `git log -S'CCHAIN(tc) = yp'` finds only the first open-source commit
  of 2016, so it has been there since then. A web search found no issue or pull request about it
  (2026-11-12). It has not been reported: nobody on the Chez team has reviewed this patch.

HOW SURE WE ARE
  - That stock Chez leaks here: high (repro above, valgrind, and the explanation in the code).
  - That this patch is correct in general: moderate. For it: after the jump the escaped C frames are
    dead, and each was locked once at the start and is unlocked once by the loop; the whole CREST suite
    (228 lit tests, 3 gtest binaries) passes on Linux and valgrind reports no invalid free.
    Not tested: call/cc re-entering a frame, threads, engines (timer interrupts), an error raised
    while unwinding, Windows.
  - If it is wrong, the failure is a crash or a double free, which is worse than the leak.

WHY IT WAS NOT TO BE USED ON WINDOWS
  The list of active C calls is also what Windows may use when the operating system unwinds the stack
  (SEH). The comment in S_return says "Windows might need the full chain for unwind handling". The
  patch changes WHEN the list is shortened, and we cannot test Windows.

